export default {
  'accepted-applicant-to-student-handover': [
    ['Name the milestone the handover is meant to achieve', `Before transferring an accepted application, agree on the meaning of enrolment readiness. An offer decision, the family's response and the school's preparation for the first day may be recorded by different people. Treating them as one event can make a dashboard show a student as ready while important arrangements remain unresolved.

Write a completion condition for the handover that each receiving team can understand. For example, the school may need a verified student identity, an approved joining date, a confirmed class allocation and an identified owner for any remaining condition. The actual conditions must follow the school's process, not an arbitrary checklist copied from another institution.

Keep the acceptance decision visible as its own evidence. The enrolment handover should not reopen that decision informally, but neither should it hide an unresolved condition attached to it. If a condition prevents readiness, identify the responsible decision-maker and the next review rather than allowing a general accepted status to stand in for a specific answer.`],
    ['Use a controlled handover record instead of a message bundle', `The receiving staff need a concise operational record, not a collection of screenshots and forwarded conversations. Link the accepted application and identify the student reference, intended year, joining date, class, section and relevant outstanding conditions. A reference to the authoritative application lets the next team find approved information without maintaining another independent copy.

Record which information is confirmed and which still needs a decision. A proposed section should not be presented as final merely because it appears in a message. Likewise, an expected joining date may need confirmation when a family changes its plans. The record should make uncertainty understandable rather than conceal it behind a blank field.

Identify the person coordinating the transfer and the owners of individual tasks. One coordinator can track completion without becoming the approver for academic, accounts and access decisions. This division helps staff know who can resolve a particular issue and avoids passing every question back to the counsellor who first received the enquiry.`],
    ['Confirm the identity and family relationships once', `Review the accepted applicant's identifying details through the school's authorised process before creating or linking the student record. Use the established application reference and the verified information already available. Re-keying everything from a printed form can introduce differences that later make the application and student records difficult to connect.

Check family relationships separately from student identity. A shared phone number may indicate siblings or another authorised relationship, but it is not sufficient evidence by itself to join accounts. Use the school's normal verification procedure when linking a parent or guardian to an additional student.

Avoid creating another family account simply because it is quicker than checking an existing relationship. Duplicate identities can lead to separate notifications or incomplete views. At the same time, do not merge records merely because names look similar. Record unresolved identity questions for authorised review before relying on the relationship for access to student information.`],
    ['Check class placement against the actual joining period', `Class, section, academic year and joining date describe different parts of the student's placement. Review them individually. A student accepted for the next academic year should not accidentally appear in the current year's class list, while a mid-term joiner needs the actual start date reflected in the operational records that depend on it.

Confirm who may approve the placement and how a later change is communicated. Admissions may record the requested class, while an academic owner confirms the final section. Keep the provisional and confirmed states distinguishable so reception and teachers do not give families conflicting instructions.

After placement is recorded, inspect the relevant class view using an authorised account. The purpose is to confirm that the student appears in the intended population, not merely that a form saved. If the class list is prepared through an export or another process, identify when it will be refreshed so the first-day team does not use an older version.`],
    ['Pass fee information as approved facts, not promises', `The accounts handover should identify the applicable approved arrangement and any decision still awaiting review. Do not turn a counsellor's informal explanation into an unapproved concession or copy another student's fee demand because the two students are joining the same class.

Distinguish the fee structure from the creation of an individual demand and from a payment already received. These are related but separate records. The handover should make clear which stage has occurred and which task belongs to accounts. If scheduled generation is involved, ask the responsible team how the new student's joining date affects eligibility under the configured process.

For a mid-term admission, record the actual date and refer any special treatment to the authorised accounts decision-maker. This guide does not prescribe a proration or concession rule. The useful handover supplies accurate context and a decision owner, allowing accounts to apply the school's approved treatment and explain it to the family.`],
    ['Prepare the classroom team with relevant information', `The class teacher needs enough information to welcome the student and understand when responsibility begins. Confirm the student's name, class, joining date and any classroom arrangements that have been approved through the appropriate school process. Share additional support information only through the authorised route and with the people who need it for their responsibilities.

Do not copy the full admissions file into a general teacher message. Much of that information may be irrelevant to classroom readiness. A concise briefing can identify where authorised staff should obtain further context without distributing private documents widely.

Ask the receiving team to acknowledge the operational handover. The acknowledgement should establish that the student is expected and that the teacher knows where to find the relevant information. If the teacher changes before the joining date, transfer that responsibility explicitly. A completed admission record does not guarantee that the person greeting the student has received the necessary briefing.`],
    ['Verify family access without confusing delivery and usability', `If the school provides a parent-access route, establish the correct authorised recipient and explain how the family is expected to use it. A sent invitation or message does not prove that the recipient can complete the intended task. Provide the official help route for an access problem rather than asking families to share passwords with staff.

Use the school's approved procedure to verify relationships and resolve incorrect contact details. Do not give access to another person's record merely because a requester knows the student's name. Where a family already has an account for a sibling, confirm the supported linking process instead of creating an unnecessary separate identity.

Keep access readiness distinct from communication readiness. A parent may receive joining instructions through an approved channel while an account issue is still being resolved. Record the remaining action and owner so the school can support the family without delaying every unrelated part of the first-day preparation.`],
    ['Rehearse a mid-term joining scenario', `Consider a fictional student accepted for Class 6 who will join on a Wednesday after the monthly fee generation date. Admissions confirms the application decision and the actual joining date. The academic owner confirms the section, while accounts reviews the applicable fee treatment rather than treating the missed generation run as permission to create an arbitrary charge.

The teacher receives the joining date and knows that earlier sessions are outside the student's enrolment period. Reception receives the approved arrival location and contact. The family receives the same location and time, with an identified route for questions. Any parent-account issue is tracked separately with its own owner.

Before the handover is closed, the coordinator checks that each receiving team has completed or explicitly accepted its action. If the family postpones joining, the date change is passed to those teams rather than updated only in the counsellor's note. The example demonstrates why a shared milestone is useful: one change can affect several otherwise separate responsibilities.`],
    ['Keep joining-date changes under control', `A family may change the planned start date after readiness work has begun. Record the revised date through the approved process and identify the tasks affected. Class lists, teacher briefings, arrival instructions and accounts treatment may all need review, but not every change requires recreating the student record.

Preserve enough history to explain the sequence. The team should be able to distinguish a data-entry mistake from a genuine change of plan. That context can matter when investigating a premature absence message or a fee created using an earlier date.

Notify the relevant owners and confirm that each dependent process has been checked. Avoid assuming that updating one field automatically updates every external list or earlier communication. Where the system handles a dependency automatically, verify the behaviour in the school's configuration; where it does not, assign the manual follow-up explicitly.`],
    ['Handle incomplete readiness without hiding it', `A student may have an approved place while one readiness task remains unresolved. Describe that task precisely and identify whether it affects the joining decision under the school's policy. Do not use a single incomplete label that makes staff guess whether the issue concerns class allocation, an account invitation or a substantive condition.

The authorised decision-maker should determine whether the student may proceed with a documented outstanding action. A coordinator should not invent that authority merely to clear the handover queue. Record the decision, scope and responsible follow-up when an exception is approved.

Keep the remaining work visible after the first day. An unresolved task does not disappear because the student arrived successfully. The responsible team needs a review date and an expected outcome. This separates welcoming the child into the classroom from losing track of an administrative obligation that still needs an authorised resolution.`],
    ['Test the handover with a person outside admissions', `Before adopting the checklist widely, ask a receiving colleague to use a fictional application and explain the student's first-day plan. Give them the handover record without an additional verbal briefing. Check whether they can identify the confirmed section, arrival time, responsible teacher and the route for an outstanding question.

Where the colleague has to guess, improve the record or the linked instruction. Do not solve the exercise by adding another undocumented explanation that only the current admissions team knows. The purpose is to make the transfer understandable when a different person performs it next month.

Repeat the exercise with a sibling and a changed joining date. These cases test different relationships and dependencies from a straightforward new admission. Keep the exercise focused on operational readiness; it is not permission to expose real applicant documents to staff who do not need them.`],
    ['Use the first-day review to improve the next handover', `After the joining day, ask the receiving teams what information was missing or inconsistent. Reception may have lacked an updated arrival list, the teacher may have received the wrong section or accounts may have discovered an undocumented promise. Record the process issue and its owner rather than attributing every gap to the family.

Check whether the agreed preparation was actually useful. A field that nobody uses may be unnecessary, while a repeated first-day question may need a clearer place in the handover. Improve the working record based on those observations instead of making it longer after every incident.

For a Schoolment demonstration, use this journey with fictional records and ask which steps your proposed configuration supports. Observe the application link, placement, access and relevant downstream views. The goal is an explainable transition from approved applicant to prepared student, supported by people who know what they own and what remains unfinished.`],
  ],
  'admission-source-tracking-without-double-counting': [
    ['Separate a person, an applicant and an interaction', `A school can count several legitimate things during admissions. A family is a relationship group, a prospective student is an individual applicant and an interaction is a call, message or visit. The same conversation may concern two prospective students. A reliable report keeps these units distinguishable before calculating totals.

Write the unit beside each measure. “New student enquiries” should mean something different from “contact attempts completed”. Avoid a generic leads total that changes meaning between the reception register and the leadership dashboard. The definitions should be understandable to the staff collecting the information, not just the person preparing a chart.

When reviewing a number, ask which event adds one to it. If a second phone call adds another new enquiry despite being connected to the same applicant, the report measures contact volume rather than unique demand. That may still be useful, but it must be labelled accurately.`],
    ['Keep original discovery separate from later contact channels', `A family's discovery source answers how it first learned about the school, to the extent that the family knows and chooses to tell the office. A contact channel answers how the current interaction occurred. A recommendation followed by a website form and a phone call is not necessarily three separate discovery sources.

Use distinct fields or an equivalent approved record structure. Preserve the reported discovery source when later interactions occur. Record a change only when the original entry was corrected or the reporting definition explicitly allows another treatment, and keep enough context to explain that change.

Do not force a precise answer when the parent is unsure. “Not known” is more honest than assigning the enquiry to whichever campaign was running that week. The uncertainty is useful information about the limits of the report and should not be hidden to make a source chart look complete.`],
    ['Define a source list that staff can apply', `Choose categories that reflect the questions the school wants to answer. A broad category such as recommendation may be sufficient for one review, while another may need a distinction between an existing family and a community event. Avoid collecting detailed attribution that nobody uses or that staff cannot explain consistently.

Keep an approved meaning for each category. “Website” could refer to discovery through search, a direct visit after a recommendation or merely the place where an application was submitted. Decide which meaning applies before collecting the data, and keep submission channels in a separate field if needed.

Review unusual entries without silently rewriting them into a convenient category. If a new source is genuinely useful, add it through the reporting owner's process and explain how it will be treated in comparisons. Frequent ad hoc category changes make a trend difficult to interpret.`],
    ['Create a review queue for suspected duplicates', `Potential duplicates should be reviewed, not automatically discarded because two fields match. A shared phone number can legitimately belong to siblings or several applications managed by one authorised contact. Similar names can refer to different people, while the same family can use more than one contact detail.

Give reviewers the context required to decide whether records should be linked, kept separate or handled through an approved consolidation process. Preserve the application reference and relevant interaction history. The review outcome should explain the relationship without losing evidence of what was originally received.

Track unresolved cases separately in the report. If the school cannot yet determine whether two records represent one applicant, do not present the unique count as perfectly settled. A provisional count with an explained review queue is more dependable than an apparently precise total based on unrecorded guesses.`],
    ['Handle siblings without erasing separate applications', `A sibling enquiry illustrates why family and applicant counts need different definitions. One parent may ask about two classes in one conversation. The family relationship should be preserved, but each prospective student may require a separate application, placement decision and eventual outcome.

If one sibling enrols and the other does not, a family-level report and an applicant-level report will describe different results. Decide which question each report is intended to answer. A school evaluating workload may need applicant counts; a communication review may need distinct families contacted.

Do not copy a sibling's discovery answer without making the relationship clear where the reporting process requires it. The office may reasonably record a shared family source, but that should be an understood rule. It should not arise accidentally because staff duplicate a row and forget which fields describe the individual applicant.`],
    ['Set reporting periods around events, not convenient exports', `Specify the date used to include a record in a source report. The first enquiry date, application date and enrolment date refer to different stages. A file downloaded this month can contain enquiries first received earlier, so the export date is not itself a useful admission cohort definition.

Keep activity and cohort views separate. A monthly activity view might show enquiries received and visits completed during that month. A source cohort view might follow applicants first recorded during a particular period and later show their outcomes. Neither should borrow the other's denominator without explanation.

When comparisons cross admission seasons, confirm that definitions remained consistent. A new duplicate-review process can reduce counted enquiries even if family interest did not change. Record such methodological changes so leadership does not attribute every movement in a chart to a marketing campaign.`],
    ['Use a worked example with three different totals', `Imagine a fictional week with six families contacting the school. Two families enquire for two children each, so there are eight prospective students. Across forms, calls and visits, the office records thirteen interactions. The valid totals are six families, eight prospective students and thirteen interactions, provided those relationships are confirmed.

Suppose a repeated website form initially appears as a ninth prospective student. The duplicate review links it to an existing applicant and preserves the additional interaction. The corrected applicant count returns to eight; the contact history remains useful rather than being deleted to make the report neat.

If four of the eight prospective students later enrol, the applicant-cohort conversion is four divided by eight, or fifty percent. A family conversion measure needs its own rule and relationship check. It cannot be inferred from the applicant percentage because the two siblings in a family may have different outcomes.`],
    ['Do not let attribution become a claim about causation', `A reported source tells the school something about how families describe discovery. It does not prove that one campaign alone caused an admission. Families can encounter several messages and recommendations before deciding to enquire, and they may remember some of those contacts more clearly than others.

Use source data as a prompt for investigation rather than as a complete explanation of demand. A small source with several completed applications may merit attention, but its count can be unstable. A large source may produce early-stage questions that take longer to progress. Compare outcomes with their context and reporting period.

Avoid unsupported statements about guaranteed return or campaign superiority. If the school combines source data with costs, the responsible decision-makers should define the comparison and its limitations. The admissions register should supply reliable observations, not manufacture certainty that the recorded information cannot support.`],
    ['Review unknown and corrected sources openly', `Unknown source values are not merely a data-cleaning inconvenience. They show where the office lacks an answer or where the question was not asked. Keep them visible so the report does not exaggerate the share of known categories.

When correcting a source entry, record the basis of the correction. A parent clarifying an earlier answer is different from a staff member deciding that all website forms must belong to an online campaign. The first adds evidence; the second changes the classification rule and should be treated accordingly.

Review the collection process if unknown values are common. The question may be unclear, asked at an inconvenient point or irrelevant to the immediate conversation. A short, respectful question with an option not to answer can provide more useful information than an intrusive demand for precision at the start of an enquiry.`],
    ['Connect source records to outcomes without duplicating history', `A source report becomes more useful when an enquiry can be linked to the relevant application and eventual outcome. Use stable references and the school's supported record relationships rather than matching records by name after the season ends.

Preserve the original enquiry context when an application progresses. Creating a fresh disconnected record at each stage makes the source disappear or encourages staff to enter another source value from memory. A linked history lets the report follow the same applicant without counting every transition as a new opportunity.

Where historical records cannot be linked confidently, record that limitation. Do not fill missing relationships solely to improve a conversion chart. An incomplete but honest connection is preferable to attributing an enrolment to the wrong family or campaign. Review a sample of linked records before relying on the aggregated result.`],
    ['Prepare a source report that explains its own limits', `Include the reporting period, counted unit, source definition and treatment of duplicates in the report note. Show the relevant counts alongside percentages. A reader should be able to understand whether the chart describes families, applicants or interactions without asking the analyst.

Identify provisional records and unresolved source values. If the report excludes them, state the exclusion and its effect on the denominator. A percentage of known sources is not the same as a percentage of all enquiries, even when the chart labels look similar.

Keep the purpose of the report visible. Reception may use a channel view to plan response capacity, while leadership uses an applicant cohort view to understand progression. Sharing the same unlabeled chart with both groups can invite contradictory interpretations. A concise definition note prevents that confusion without overwhelming the report with technical detail.`],
    ['Share attribution at the level the review needs', `A leadership discussion about discovery sources usually needs grouped counts and definitions, not a list of every parent's contact details. Prepare the report for its audience and keep record-level investigation within the authorised admissions workflow. If a particular classification needs review, assign that case to the appropriate staff rather than circulating the full enquiry export.

Keep the reporting date on shared copies. A duplicate correction or newly recorded outcome can change the figures after an export, so an old attachment should not silently become the official season result. Identify which version supersedes earlier copies when a report is revised.

Use small-group results carefully in discussion. Even without names, detailed combinations can reveal which family is being described to people familiar with the admissions activity. Choose the level of detail needed for the decision and avoid adding private context simply to make a campaign anecdote more memorable.`],
    ['Test the collection process before the next campaign', `Use fictional cases to rehearse the records: one parent making two calls, two siblings in one form, a family with an unknown discovery source and a returning applicant from an earlier year. Ask staff to show which records they would create or link and which counters should change.

Compare the answers across reception, counsellors and the reporting owner. Disagreement indicates a definition or workflow problem to resolve before live collection expands. Write the agreed treatment into a short operating note and keep the examples available for new staff.

In a Schoolment demonstration, ask how the configured admission records support these relationships and what reporting steps remain manual. Verify the actual behaviour instead of assuming that any chart labelled leads uses the school's intended unit. Reliable source tracking begins with clear identities and consistent events, not with a more colourful dashboard.`],
  ],
  'admission-visit-slot-capacity-planning': [
    ['Describe the visit the school is actually offering', `A visit can be a private appointment, a group orientation, a campus tour or a combination of activities. Define that format before deciding how many bookings to accept. Families should know whether they will meet a counsellor individually or join a scheduled presentation with other visitors.

Identify the beginning and end of the visit from the family's point of view. Arrival checks, waiting, movement between buildings and a final conversation all consume time even when the formal presentation is short. A calendar based only on presentation time can leave reception managing an unexpected queue.

Keep the description accurate when the format changes. If the school combines appointments into a group event, update the confirmation rather than allowing families to arrive expecting private meetings. Capacity planning works best when the offered experience, staff plan and parent instructions describe the same arrangement.`],
    ['Identify the limiting resource for each stage', `List the resources required at each point in the visit. Reception may have a limited waiting area, counsellors may handle one family at a time and a tour may depend on a staff escort. A vacant calendar slot does not establish that all these resources are available together.

Find the stage that constrains the whole experience. Adding another tour group will not solve a bottleneck at the individual counselling desk. Likewise, adding a counsellor may not help if the only available tour route conflicts with an examination activity.

Use the actual staffing and facilities agreed for that day. Do not rely on an employee being free because their teaching timetable looks empty; they may have another duty. Record the owner who confirms each resource so a booking decision is based on a coordinated plan rather than separate assumptions.`],
    ['Include preparation and hand-back time', `A counsellor needs time to review the next enquiry and record useful notes after a meeting. A tour guide may need to return to the starting point or prepare materials. If appointments are placed immediately back to back, those activities either disappear or push delays into later slots.

Build reasonable buffers from the school's observed experience and review them after actual visits. A buffer is not wasted capacity when it supports an accurate handover or prevents several families waiting at once. The appropriate duration depends on the visit format and the work involved.

Do not remove every gap to make the booking calendar appear full. First identify why the gap exists. If the school changes the format or provides separate support for preparation, the capacity may change, but the revised plan should be tested rather than assumed.`],
    ['Keep one authoritative capacity record', `When reception and counsellors can both accept bookings, they need a shared view of the remaining places. Two independent spreadsheets can each show the last slot as available and produce an overbooking without either staff member knowingly exceeding capacity.

Agree which record governs confirmation and who may alter capacity. A request received through another channel should be entered or checked against that record before being promised to the family. Avoid treating an informal message to a staff member as a confirmed appointment unless the booking process recognises it.

If the booking tool does not prevent conflicts automatically, define the manual check clearly. The person confirming the appointment should verify the current allocation and record the confirmation immediately. The school can then investigate an overbooking through a visible sequence instead of trying to reconstruct several private conversations.`],
    ['Distinguish requested, held and confirmed places', `A family asking about a time has not necessarily accepted it. If the school temporarily holds a place while awaiting a response, define how that hold is recorded and when it is reviewed. Do not leave an indefinite reservation that staff cannot distinguish from a confirmed visit.

The confirmation event should have a clear meaning, such as an agreed date and slot communicated through the approved channel. Record any relevant group-size information needed for the format. The purpose is to plan the visit, not to gather unrelated personal details about every attendee.

When a hold is released, follow the agreed communication process. Avoid offering the same place to another family while the first family reasonably believes it is still reserved. Clear states protect both the capacity plan and the credibility of the school's messages.`],
    ['Make arrival instructions part of the booking', `Provide the date, arrival window, meeting point and official contact route in the confirmation. If visitors need a particular entrance or check-in process, explain it in language a first-time family can understand. An internal room code may be familiar to staff but unhelpful at the gate.

Keep reception and the relevant arrival team aligned with those instructions. A parent should not be directed to a different location because the staff list came from an earlier version of the plan. Assign one owner to communicate changes that affect the visit journey.

Ask how the school will handle an unexpected delay or a family arriving early. The answer should be workable within the agreed resources. Do not solve every early arrival by starting the next appointment immediately if that displaces a preparation task or another confirmed family.`],
    ['Plan for stated access needs through an appropriate route', `Families may ask about the route, waiting arrangements or another requirement that affects their ability to attend. Provide an official way to raise those questions before the visit. Share only the information needed with the staff responsible for the arrangement.

Do not promise an arrangement until the relevant owner has confirmed it. A counsellor may be able to receive the request without having authority to change a facility or staffing plan. Record the question and response so the family receives accurate information rather than a well-intended assumption.

Include the confirmed arrangement in the operational briefing where appropriate. Keep sensitive context restricted; reception may need to know which route to use without receiving unnecessary personal details. The aim is to make the offered visit clear and workable, not to turn the booking form into a broad collection of private information.`],
    ['Use a realistic Saturday planning example', `Imagine a fictional school with two counsellors available for individual meetings and one staff member leading tours. Each family needs both a meeting and a tour. Four private meetings cannot all start at the same time merely because the tour guide can take a group afterwards.

The school can stagger the meetings and coordinate tour departures, or clearly offer a group orientation followed by scheduled individual conversations. Either approach needs a timetable that accounts for the actual sequence. Families should receive the format that the school has planned, not a vague invitation to arrive at ten.

After the session, compare planned and actual start times and record where queues formed. If meetings regularly run longer because parents bring complex questions, adjust the format or allocation. Do not simply assume that every counsellor should work faster. The plan needs to reflect the service the school is choosing to provide.`],
    ['Handle rescheduling without inflating demand', `A rescheduled visit is normally a change to an existing booking, not a new family enquiry. Preserve the relationship to the original applicant or family record so the admission report does not count repeated arrangements as additional demand.

Release the original slot through the authoritative capacity process and confirm the replacement only after checking availability. Record whether the family or school requested the change where that distinction helps review. Avoid leaving both appointments active because the new booking was created without resolving the earlier one.

If the school changes the date, communicate the revised arrangement and check whether the family accepts it. A new date written into a calendar does not establish agreement. The unresolved response should remain visible so reception does not prepare for an appointment that the family never confirmed.`],
    ['Define late-arrival and no-show handling', `Agree what staff should do when a family arrives after its planned start. Depending on the format, the school may be able to accommodate the visit, offer a later opening or reschedule. The actual options should protect the experience of other confirmed visitors as well as the arriving family.

Do not let every employee invent a different response at the desk. Provide a decision owner and a respectful explanation of the available arrangement. Where the visit is shortened or changed, make that clear rather than implying that the original full experience is still being delivered.

Record a no-show separately from a cancellation or a visit that was rearranged. Before drawing conclusions, check whether the school sent accurate instructions and whether a reschedule was recorded elsewhere. The category should describe the observed event, not a judgment about how interested the family is.`],
    ['Protect school activities when approving visitor windows', `Visit capacity must account for the normal work of the school. A classroom demonstration, specialist-room tour or examination period may have restrictions determined by the responsible staff. Confirm the route and timing before promising access to a particular activity.

Avoid treating a tour itinerary as permanent when the school calendar changes. A special event or room closure can affect a previously workable route. The visit coordinator needs a current source for those changes and a way to update the offered experience.

If part of the visit becomes unavailable, decide whether to provide an alternative explanation, change the route or reschedule through the approved process. Tell families what to expect. An accurate, modest description is preferable to advertising an activity that the staff cannot responsibly deliver on the day.`],
    ['Measure capacity through experience, not booking totals alone', `A fully booked calendar can still represent a poorly managed day if families wait without information and counsellors have no time to record outcomes. Review actual start delays, visit duration, cancellations and the completion of follow-up notes alongside the number of confirmed places.

Keep the measures clearly defined. A booked visit, an attended visit and a completed counselling conversation are separate events. The school should not use the largest available number as a general measure of success.

Ask staff which constraint caused the most disruption and examine representative cases before changing the limit. A longer arrival queue might reflect unclear instructions rather than insufficient counsellor capacity. A useful review identifies the process change that would improve the next session, not just a target for more bookings.`],
    ['Publish the checked plan and retain its owner', `Before the visit day, confirm the final allocations, staff responsibilities, route and parent instructions. Make the current version identifiable to reception and counsellors. If a late change occurs, update the same authoritative record and notify the people whose tasks are affected.

Keep a short closing review after the session. Record unfinished parent questions with owners and next actions rather than leaving them in a guide's memory. Capacity planning succeeds when the visit produces a clear next step as well as a completed appointment.

When evaluating Schoolment's available admission tools, use a fictional day with rescheduling, overlapping requests and a changed staff assignment. Ask which conflicts the configured system handles and which need a coordinator's check. The relevant result is a visit plan that people can execute, not merely a calendar that accepts another entry.`],
  ],
  'school-admission-statuses-explained': [
    ['Give a status one meaning across the school', `A status is useful only when different staff interpret it consistently. If reception uses application complete to mean that a form was received while the reviewer uses it to mean that all required material is ready, the same label can produce incompatible decisions.

Write the meaning in plain language and identify the event that makes it true. The definition should describe observable progress rather than enthusiasm or likelihood. A parent saying they are interested is not the same as submitting an application, and an offer being sent is not the same as the family accepting it.

Keep the definition close to the operating process. Staff should not have to search a lengthy manual for the meaning of a routine label. A short reference with examples and an identified owner makes it easier to resolve uncertainty without inventing local interpretations.`],
    ['Distinguish stages from tasks and outcomes', `A stage describes where the application stands in the process. A task describes work still needed, such as reviewing a replacement document. An outcome records a decision or final disposition. Trying to encode all three in one status field can create a long list that nobody applies reliably.

For example, an application can remain under review while a particular clarification task is assigned. The school does not necessarily need a new stage called waiting for phone call about document. Keep the actionable task visible without making the main pipeline impossible to understand.

Similarly, record the reason for a withdrawal or closure through the appropriate field or note rather than creating a separate status for every circumstance. The exact design depends on the tools available, but the conceptual distinction helps staff avoid using one label as a substitute for several different facts.`],
    ['Define the evidence required to enter each stage', `For every stage, identify the evidence that must exist before the record can enter it. A scheduled visit should have an agreed date and arrangement. An application ready for review should satisfy the school's defined completeness conditions. A decision stage should refer to the authorised decision, not a counsellor's prediction.

The evidence may be a linked record or a documented confirmation rather than an attachment. Do not require extra documents merely to make the workflow appear formal. Use the information necessary to establish the milestone under the school's process.

Where the evidence is incomplete, keep the record in a state that describes that limitation and assign the next action. Moving it forward to remove it from a queue may improve a chart temporarily, but it makes the status less useful to the next person who relies on it.`],
    ['Define the exit condition as well as the entry condition', `A stage should not become a permanent holding area with no clear next step. Specify what must happen for the record to progress, return for correction or close. This helps staff distinguish a legitimate wait from forgotten work.

An offer-sent stage, for example, may wait for the family's response or an authorised extension decision. It should not automatically become enrolled because time has passed. The exit rule needs to reflect what the school actually requires to confirm the place.

Keep exceptional exits explicit. A family may withdraw while an application is under review, or the school may need a correction that moves the record back to a preparation stage. The status history should explain the event rather than force every application through an artificial sequence simply to keep the pipeline orderly.`],
    ['Assign transition authority to responsibilities', `Decide who may record each transition. The person who receives a form may be authorised to acknowledge receipt without being authorised to approve admission. A counsellor may communicate an offer without having the authority to create that decision.

Map those responsibilities to the actual roles and permissions available in the system. Where the software does not enforce every boundary, the office still needs an approved procedure. Do not assume that the presence of an editable dropdown gives every user decision-making authority.

Provide a route for legitimate corrections and leave cover. If the authorised reviewer is absent, use the school's documented delegation process rather than sharing an account or asking another staff member to record a decision under the wrong identity. The transition history should remain understandable when reviewed later.`],
    ['Keep parent responses separate from school decisions', `A school decision and a family's response are distinct events. An approved offer may await acceptance; an accepted offer may still have defined enrolment conditions. Record those distinctions so the school does not count a place as occupied or ready for joining before the relevant milestone has occurred.

Use the communication record to establish what the family has actually confirmed. A message being delivered does not show acceptance of its contents. If the response is unclear, ask for clarification through the approved channel instead of inferring a decision from silence.

When a family requests more time, record the authorised treatment and revised response date. Preserve the original event and the extension context where required. The pipeline should show the current position accurately without treating the extension as a second offer or a fresh application.`],
    ['Use a transition example with a correction loop', `Consider a fictional applicant whose form has been received but whose required document is unreadable. The record remains in the preparation stage with a specific correction task. When the replacement arrives, the task changes to school review; receipt alone does not establish that the application is ready for a decision.

The reviewer accepts the replacement and confirms completeness, allowing the application to enter the defined review stage. Later, an authorised decision is recorded and an offer is sent. The family asks for a clarification before accepting. That question becomes a task linked to the offer stage rather than an invented acceptance event.

The example contains progress, waiting and correction without requiring staff to pretend the process moved only forward. A useful status model can represent real work while preserving the meaning of each milestone.`],
    ['Preserve reasons for reversals and corrections', `A record may need to move back because information was entered incorrectly or a requirement was not actually satisfied. Provide a way to record the reason and the authorised correction. Otherwise a reversal can look like unexplained inconsistency to the next staff member.

Distinguish a correction to an erroneous status from a genuine later event. If an offer was never sent, correcting an accidental offer-sent label is different from recording a withdrawal after a real offer. The history should support that distinction to the extent provided by the school's system and procedure.

Avoid deleting supporting communication simply to make the current stage appear cleaner. The school may need the sequence to answer a family question or review a process issue. Keep the necessary history through the approved record-management process rather than replacing it with an unexplained final label.`],
    ['Review stalled records using the reason for waiting', `Time in a stage can identify records worth inspecting, but it does not explain why they are there. An application awaiting an agreed response date is different from one with no assigned reviewer. Review the next action, owner and dependency before deciding that a delay represents poor performance.

Use a short exception view for records missing those elements. The first question is whether someone knows what should happen next. If the answer is no, assign clarification rather than simply changing the status to another waiting label.

When the school owes an update, communicate it through the agreed route. If the family has been asked for information, check whether it has already arrived elsewhere before sending another reminder. The status review should improve the actual work, not produce a routine message disconnected from the latest record.`],
    ['Make reports use the same definitions as staff', `A dashboard should count the stages as defined in the operating process. If a report treats offer sent as confirmed admission while staff use it to mean awaiting response, the disagreement will make the numbers difficult to trust.

Document which statuses contribute to each measure and the reporting date used. A current snapshot answers how many records occupy each stage now; a transition report answers how many entered a stage during a period. These are different questions and should not share an unexplained label.

Review a sample behind the totals. A chart can be technically consistent with the stored labels while the labels themselves were entered incorrectly. The sample helps the reporting owner determine whether the pipeline reflects actual milestones or merely the easiest choices available in a form.`],
    ['Retire confusing labels without rewriting history casually', `If the school changes its status model, plan how current records will be interpreted. A former label may contain several different situations that now need separate treatment. Do not map all of them into one new stage without reviewing the underlying meaning.

Record the effective date of the new definitions and brief staff who use the pipeline. Identify whether historical reports retain the earlier interpretation or are presented with an explained conversion. The appropriate treatment depends on the school's reporting needs and available system history.

Keep the change proportionate. A clearer description may resolve confusion without requiring a completely new workflow. The goal is consistency, not frequent redesign. Review examples with the team before changing labels so the new version addresses actual ambiguity rather than introducing another set of unfamiliar terms.`],
    ['Train with borderline cases rather than only perfect applications', `Use fictional cases to ask staff which stage applies and why. Include a visit requested but not confirmed, a replacement document received but not reviewed, an offer awaiting a response and a family withdrawing during review. These cases reveal misunderstandings that a simple end-to-end demonstration can miss.

Ask staff to identify the next task and the person who may authorise the transition. A correct status choice is only part of the exercise; the record should also support the next useful action. Discuss disagreements using the approved definitions rather than personal preferences about labels.

Keep the agreed examples with the operating note. New counsellors and temporary staff can use them to understand the process without learning through avoidable mistakes on live applications. Update the examples when a real process change affects the meaning of a stage.`],
    ['Validate the configured workflow before relying on the pipeline', `In a Schoolment review, demonstrate the actual status fields, role permissions and available history with a fictional application. Check ordinary progression, a return for correction and a withdrawal. Confirm which controls are enforced by configuration and which depend on the office procedure.

Inspect the resulting reports after those changes. The counts should match the agreed definitions, and unresolved tasks should remain visible through the relevant workflow. Do not assume that a label appearing in a menu establishes the full transition logic the school needs.

A dependable admission pipeline is a shared language for work already completed and work still owed. It helps the next staff member understand the record and helps leadership interpret progress without exaggeration. That value comes from clear evidence and consistent use, not from choosing the most optimistic description for every application.`],
  ],
};
